      "sec_sub": "A hosted bot platform holds your identity, your contacts and your message history. A self-hosted studio holds the same files for you instead.",
      "sec_title": "What stays on your machine",
      "sg_1_d": "STUDIO_ADMIN_TOKEN is read from the environment or .env and compared in constant time. Bot tokens and the model API key stay in the SQLite ledger on your disk and are masked in every API response. At rest the ledger is not encrypted: the 0600 file mode, the 0700 data directory and the loopback bind are what protect it. Only cold-backup bundles are encrypted, with AES-GCM under a key derived by scrypt.",
      "sg_1_t": "Where the keys live",
      "sg_2_d": "Partly implemented. agent_task_grants decides which bot may borrow which read-only capability: default-deny, wildcard target only on purpose, with hop and concurrency caps. What is missing is a per-tool scope in the plugin dispatcher, which still runs plugins by priority and checks no scope field.",
      "sg_2_t": "Capability tokens",
      "sg_3_d": "Messages, rule hits, plugin errors, webhook changes, orchestrator tasks and A2A or guest actions are written to the ledger and readable in the console. Admin actions do not yet have their own audit table, and there is no export format for an auditor.",
      "sg_3_t": "Audit trail",
      "sg_4_d": "Orchestrator grants can be toggled off or deleted from the console, and a webhook target can be cleared there. Admin and bot tokens still rotate by editing .env and restarting; there is no self-service rotate or revoke endpoint.",
      "sg_4_t": "Revoking access",
      "sg_headline": "The permission boundary, item by item",
